.SH NAME
csv2xlsx \- Convert CSV to Excel 2007+
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\& csv2xlsx [options] [file.csv]
\&
\& csv2xlsx test.csv
\&
\& csv2xlsx \-f \-o merged.xlsx \-m foobar*.csv
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This tool converts CSV data to Excel\-2007+. It can convert the CSV into a
single sheet, merge CSV files into multiple sheets in a workbook or split
a single CSV into multiple sheets.
.PP
The tool supports encoding, formula handle, date conversion and some more.
.SH OPTIONS
.IX Header "OPTIONS"
.TP 2
\&\-s S
.IX Item "-s S"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-sep=S
.IX Item "--sep=S"
.PD
use \f(CR\*(C`S\*(C'\fR as separator character, auto\-detect, default = \f(CR\*(C`,\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
The literal string \f(CR\*(C`tab\*(C'\fR is allowed.
.TP 2
\&\-q Q
.IX Item "-q Q"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-quo=Q
.IX Item "--quo=Q"
.PD
use \f(CR\*(C`Q\*(C'\fR as quotation character, auto\-detect, default = \f(CR\*(C`"\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
The literal string \f(CR\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR is allowed to disable quotation.
.TP 2
\&\-e E
.IX Item "-e E"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-esc=E
.IX Item "--esc=E"
.PD
use \f(CR\*(C`E\*(C'\fR as escape character, auto\-detect, default = \f(CR\*(C`"\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
The literal string \f(CR\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR is allowed to disable escapes.
.TP 2
\&\-w W
.IX Item "-w W"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-width=W
.IX Item "--width=W"
.PD
Set column width. Default is to auto\-size per column per sheet with a
minimum width of \f(CR4\fR.
.TP 2
\&\-o FILE.xlsx
.IX Item "-o FILE.xlsx"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-x FILE.xlsx
.IX Item "-x FILE.xlsx"
.TP 2
\&\-\-out=FILE.xlsx
.IX Item "--out=FILE.xlsx"
.PD
Specify the output filename. Default it the same name as the input\-file
where the trailing \f(CR\*(C`.csv\*(C'\fR will be replaced with \f(CR\*(C`.xlsx\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
If a filename can not be automatically generated, it will default to
\&\f(CR\*(C`csv2xlsx.xlsx\*(C'\fR.
.TP 2
\&\-d format
.IX Item "-d format"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-date\-fmt=format
.IX Item "--date-fmt=format"
.PD
Use \f(CR\*(C`format\*(C'\fR as date formats. Default = \f(CR\*(C`dd\-mm\-yyyy\*(C'\fR.
.TP 2
\&\-D range
.IX Item "-D range"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-date\-col=range
.IX Item "--date-col=range"
.PD
Only convert dates in columns \f(CR\*(C`range\*(C'\fR. Default is everywhere.
.Sp
Ranges are numeric, where the first column has index \f(CR1\fR. A range is a
definition of sections joined by \f(CR\*(C`,\*(C'\fRs. A section is either a single column
or a start/finish\-pair joined with a dash. A missing finish index on the
last segment is an open range (till the end of the line).
.Sp
If the range is \f(CR0\fR, date conversion is disabled.
.Sp
.Vb 3
\& \-D 0
\& \-D 1,2,6
\& \-D 2,4\-8,12\-
.Ve
.TP 2
\&\-C format
.IX Item "-C format"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-curr\-fmt=format
.IX Item "--curr-fmt=format"
.PD
Use \f(CR\*(C`format\*(C'\fR as currency formats for currency <C>, no default.
.Sp
.Vb 1
\& \-C \*(Aq$:### ### ##0.00\*(Aq
.Ve
.TP 2
\&\-\-font=Calibri
.IX Item "--font=Calibri"
You can set the default font. The XLSX writer used sets the default font
face to \f(CR\*(C`Calibri\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
.Vb 1
\& \-\-font=Arial
.Ve
.Sp
There are 3 default shortcuts
.Sp
.Vb 3
\& \-\-font=M
\& \-\-font=D
\& \-\-font=DM
.Ve
.Sp
Setting the font to \f(CR\*(C`mono\*(C'\fR, \f(CR\*(C`DejaVu Sans\*(C'\fR, or \f(CR\*(C`DejaVu Sans Mono\*(C'\fR.
.TP 2
\&\-\-fs=10
.IX Item "--fs=10"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-font\-size=10
.IX Item "--font-size=10"
.PD
Optional set the default font\-size to anything other than the default 10.
Must be a positive integer. No checks.
.TP 2
\&\-f
.IX Item "-f"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-force
.IX Item "--force"
.PD
Force usage of the output file if it already exists (unlink before use).
.TP 2
\&\-F
.IX Item "-F"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-formulas
.IX Item "--formulas"
.PD
Allow formula\*(Aqs. Otherwise fields starting with an equal sign \f(CR\*(C`=\*(C'\fR are
forced to string type.
.Sp
There are several shortcuts here to specify different behavior:
.RS 2
.TP 2
\&\-\-Fa=a
.IX Item "--Fa=a"
Define formula action: \f(CR\*(C`none\*(C'\fR/\f(CR\*(C`die\*(C'\fR/\f(CR\*(C`croak\*(C'\fR/\f(CR\*(C`diag\*(C'\fR/\f(CR\*(C`empty\*(C'\fR/\f(CR\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR
.TP 2
\&\-\-Ft
.IX Item "--Ft"
Formula\*(Aqs will be stored as text (formula actions: none)
.TP 2
\&\-\-Fd
.IX Item "--Fd"
Formula\*(Aqs will cause a die
.TP 2
\&\-\-Fc
.IX Item "--Fc"
Formula\*(Aqs will cause a croak
.TP 2
\&\-\-FD
.IX Item "--FD"
Formula\*(Aqs will cause a warning (this is the default)
.TP 2
\&\-\-Fe
.IX Item "--Fe"
Formula\*(Aqs will be replaced by the empty string
.TP 2
\&\-\-Fu
.IX Item "--Fu"
Formula\*(Aqs will be replaced with an undefined cell
.RE
.RS 2
.RE
.TP 2
\&\-u
.IX Item "-u"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-utf\-8
.IX Item "--utf-8"
.TP 2
\&\-\-utf8
.IX Item "--utf8"
.PD
CSV is UTF\-8 encoded. Likely not needed, as most is auto\-detected.
.TP 2
\&\-\-de
.IX Item "--de"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-fix\-utf8
.IX Item "--fix-utf8"
.PD
Attempt to fix double\-encoded UTF\-8. e.g. \f(CR\*(C`\ex{00c3}\ex{00ab}\*(C'\fR should have
been \f(CR\*(C`\ex0100}\*(C'\fR. YMMV.
.TP 2
\&\-m
.IX Item "-m"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-merge
.IX Item "--merge"
.PD
Merge multiple CSV\*(Aqs into a single Excel (separate sheets).
.Sp
With this option, the option \f(CR\*(C`\-o\*(C'\fR is required. All arguments should be
existing files. Piping is not supported.
.TP 2
\&\-\-skip\-empty
.IX Item "--skip-empty"
When merging CSV files with \f(CR\*(C`\-m\*(C'\fR, skip empty files. The default is to exit
with an error message.
.TP 2
\&\-L N
.IX Item "-L N"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-row\-limit=N
.IX Item "--row-limit=N"
.PD
Limit export to \f(CR\*(C`N\*(C'\fR rows.
.TP 2
\&\-S CxP
.IX Item "-S CxP"
.PD 0
.TP 2
\&\-\-split=CxP
.IX Item "--split=CxP"
.PD
When dealing with big CSV datasets, this option enables you to split the
data over several sheets. When all the \f(CR\*(C`\-S\*(C'\fR options match in a single row,
that row will be the first row of a new sheet. (see also \f(CR\*(C`\-\-sl=C\*(C'\fR)
.RS 2
.TP 2
C
.IX Item "C"
The column that should be examined. \f(CR\*(C`A\*(C'\fR = \f(CR1\fR. If lower case, the value
of that column is matched case insensitive when appropriate.
.Sp
.Vb 3
\& \-S 7=ab      Column G is literal "ab"
\& \-S G=ab      Column G is literal "ab"
\& \-S g=ab      Column G is literal "ab", or "aB", or "Ab", or "AB"
.Ve
.TP 2
x
.IX Item "x"
Defines the operation on the column
.RS 2
.TP 2
=
Literal match
.Sp
.Vb 2
\& \-S G=ab      Column G is literal "ab"
\& \-S g=ab      Column G is literal "ab", or "aB", or "Ab", or "AB"
.Ve
.TP 2
/
Regex match
.Sp
.Vb 2
\& \-S G/b[a\-z]  Column G matches /b[a\-z]/
\& \-S g/b[a\-z]  Column G matches /b[a\-z]/i
.Ve
.TP 2
u U
.IX Item "u U"
Check for defined
.Sp
.Vb 2
\& \-S Gu        Column G is undefined
\& \-S GU        Column G is defined
.Ve
.Sp
Similar for emptiness
.Sp
.Vb 2
\& \-S G=        Column G is defined but empty
\& \-S G/.       Column G is defined and not empty
.Ve
.TP 2
< >
Compare. If the value in the column is defined compare. If the values only
holds digits, do a numeric compare, otherwise do a string compare.
.Sp
.Vb 2
\& \-S G<42      Column G is defined and less than 42 (numeric)
\& \-S G<ab      Column G is defined and less than 42 (string)
\&
\& \-S G>42      Column G is defined and greater than 42 (numeric)
\& \-S G>ab      Column G is defined and greater than 42 (string)
.Ve
.RE
.RS 2
.RE
.TP 2
P
.IX Item "P"
pattern or literal string. Quotation might be required differing per OS and
shell\-environment.
.RE
.RS 2
.RE
.TP 2
\&\-\-sl=C
.IX Item "--sl=C"
When splitting with \f(CR\*(C`\-S\*(C'\fR / \f(CR\*(C`\-\-split\*(C'\fR, if all criteria match and a new
sheet is to be created, use the value in column \f(CR\*(C`C\*(C'\fR of the matching row
as the new sheet label.
.TP 2
